From f6c6f3cb5ee902c2ab3f77b79b3ba38db07831e3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Michiel <367462+meghuizen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 10:16:34 +0200
Subject: [PATCH net-next 1/1] udp: wake a drained batch of datagrams with
 one sk_data_ready call

Since commit b650bf0977d3 ("udp: remove busylock and add per NUMA
queues"), __udp_enqueue_schedule_skb() moves every skb queued on the
per-NUMA llist into sk_receive_queue under one lock, and then calls
sk_data_ready() once per skb so that up to nb threads blocked in
recvmsg() (exclusive waiters) are woken. Each of those calls also runs
every non-exclusive waiter on the socket, such as an epoll or poll
entry, and takes the smp_mb() in skwq_has_sleeper() and the waitqueue
lock: nb times for one queue that became non-empty.

Add sock_data_ready_nr(). For a socket with the default sk_data_ready
it issues one sync wakeup with nr_exclusive = nb, through a new
__wake_up_sync_key_nr(). Up to nb exclusive waiters are still woken;
non-exclusive waiters are called once. A socket whose sk_data_ready has
been replaced (sockmap, sunrpc) or that has O_ASYNC set keeps the
per-skb loop.

nb > 1 only happens when several CPUs enqueue to one socket at the same
time. Measured on the 6.18 host kernel, which has the per-NUMA queues
but not this patch: N threads, each on its own CPU, send 64-byte
datagrams over loopback to one socket drained from epoll_wait(), 3 s,
one run each; bpftrace counts sock_def_readable() calls per
__udp_enqueue_schedule_skb() call. "Removed" is the number of calls
this patch would not make (calls minus drains).

  senders  sk_data_ready calls  drains  drains with nb>1  removed
     1           27071          27071          0              0
     2           87270          86612        616            658  (0.8%)
     6          310765         292849      13416          17916  (5.8%)
    12          326193         274199      29147          51994 (15.9%)
    24          299610         245418      22598          54192 (18.1%)

At 12 and 24 senders the receive buffer overflowed (about 1/3 of
datagrams dropped). The kprobes lengthen each enqueue, which may
itself make batches larger than they would be without tracing.

On a patched kernel (x86_64 guest, nested KVM, 16 vCPUs, this patch
alone on the base commit, 2 boots, base: exactly 1.00 in 9 boots),
same test drained from epoll_wait(), ep_poll_callback() calls per
datagram received (ftrace function profiler):

  senders   base    patched
     1      1.00    1.00, 1.00
     6      1.00    0.91, 0.87
    12      1.00    0.47, 0.45

With unpaced senders the patched kernel's senders run faster, because
they make fewer wakeup calls, and at 12 senders they overflow the
receive buffer more often than on the base kernel.  With the senders
paced to the same total rate on both kernels (50k-200k datagrams/s, 12
senders, 3 boots each, interleaved), the patched kernel dropped 30 of
13.5M datagrams and the base kernel 61000, all in runs where the host
stalled; receiver CPU time per datagram was the same (0.7-0.85 us).
With 6 or 2 senders and 4 threads blocked in recv(), no datagram went
unaccounted and no wakeup arrived late (2 boots, 4 runs each).

sock_def_readable() compiles to the same code as before apart from
passing nr_exclusive = 1 (64 instructions before and after).
Build-tested (W=1) at this commit.

Generated code, built kernel (x86_64): __udp_enqueue_schedule_skb()
calls sock_data_ready_nr() once per drained batch instead of calling
sock_def_readable(), or the indirect callback, once per datagram; it
grows from 257 to 263 instructions, and the new sock_data_ready_nr() is
88.

Assisted-by: Claude bpftrace
---
Impact: medium -- a UDP socket that receives from several CPUs at once
(one unconnected socket behind RSS/RPS, or a flood); a connected
single-flow socket normally sees nb = 1 and is unaffected.

Notes for reviewers:
- nb == 0 (every skb of the batch dropped by udp_rmem_schedule()) is
  handled: sock_data_ready_nr() returns without a wakeup, as the old
  loop did. Passing 0 to __wake_up_common() would wake every waiter.
- Exclusive waiters: __wake_up_common() with nr_exclusive = nb wakes the
  same set as nb calls with nr_exclusive = 1 did: entries whose wake
  function returns 0 (task already running, EPOLLEXCLUSIVE epitem with
  no waiter) are skipped in both cases.
- UDP sockets with a replaced sk_data_ready in this tree:
  sk_psock_verdict_data_ready() (sockmap), xs_data_ready() (sunrpc
  client), svc_data_ready() (sunrpc server). They keep the old loop,
  re-reading sk_data_ready each iteration as before. espintcp, tls,
  kcm, nvme-tcp, ceph etc. override TCP sockets and do not reach this
  code. TCP and other callers of sock_def_readable() are unchanged.
- One visible difference remains for the default callback: the
  sk_data_ready tracepoint fires once per drained batch instead of once
  per skb.
- Test programs (udp_batch_wake, udp_rate_wake) and raw results can
  be provided.

